2.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {B2}

B2 is the only first point that creates an immediate and irrevocable atari on the C1–C2 group while simultaneously cutting the communication between that group and the A2 and B3–B4 groups.

Before White moves C1–C2 has exactly the two liberties B1 and B2. B2 is the common liberty of all three black components: A2 loses B2, B3–B4 loses B2, and C1–C2 loses B2. After 1.W B2 we get

* C1–C2 → {B1}
* A2 → {A1,A3}
* B3–B4 → {A3,A4}

C1–C2 is now in atari on B1 and Black cannot avoid playing there if it wants the group to live. If 1…B1, the group C1–C2–B1 has the single liberty A1 because C2 is already blocked by White at B2, C3 and D2. 2.W A1 captures C1–C2–B1 and the capturing stone is alive on B1. The remaining A2 and B3–B4 formations are then tied to the A1–A3–A4 corner and are killed by the standard squeeze: 3.B A3 4.W A4 or 3.B A4 4.W A3.

If Black does not answer B1, 2.W B1 captures C1–C2 at once. The capturing stone lives on A1 on the next move and the A2–B3–B4 complex is left with only A1,A3,A4 as breathing room, which is insufficient once the corner is taken.

1.W B1 is not a net. First B1 gives Black the immediate connecting move 1…B2 which merges A2, B3–B4 and C1–C2 into one large group with liberties A1,A3,A4 and gives the newly played White B1 stone only the liberty A1. Black can then recapture on A1 or defend on A3/A4, and the White B1 stone is the one in atari. The forced kill only starts with the removal of the shared liberty at B2.

