PUBLIC RESPONSE: {B2}

B2 is the net point because it is the only first move that simultaneously puts C1–C2 into immediate atari and cuts the shared breathing room of A2 and B3–B4.

Before White moves
* C1–C2 : {B1,B2}
* A2    : {A1,A3,B2}
* B3–B4 : {A3,A4,B2}

1.W B2 changes the liberty map to
* C1–C2 : {B1}
* A2    : {A1,A3}
* B3–B4 : {A3,A4}

C1–C2 is now in atari on B1 and Black has no useful alternative. If 1…B1, the three stones C1–C2–B1 become one group whose only free point is A1, since B2 is now White, C3 blocks the north side of C2 and D1/D2 block the east side. 2.W A1 removes C1,C2,B1 and the capturing stone is alive on the newly emptied B1.

If Black tries anything other than B1, 2.W B1 captures C1–C2 immediately and the capturing stone is kept alive on A1 on the next move. In either case A2 and B3–B4 are left with the reduced set {A3,A4} plus A1 after the corner is occupied, which is a standard A3/A4 squeeze.

By contrast 1.W B1 is a self-defeating first point. 1.W B1 2.B B2 connects A2, B3–B4 and C1–C2 into one large Black group with liberties A1,A3,A4 and the newly played White B1 now has only A1 to breathe, i.e. White is in atari. Black can recapture on A1 or defend on A3/A4 and the net is lost. No other White first move reduces C1–C2 to a single liberty while also denying B2 to A2 and B3–B4, so B2 is the unique forced-kill initiator.

